  "answers": {
    "q1_reproduce_rho90": "**No.** Per-split utility gates cannot reproduce rho0.90 without an explicit rho cutoff. The best utility rule (rule1_purity_gain) accepts only 1.3 splits/run vs 239.7 for rho0.90 (2.3 vs 77.8 balls). Elevated macro F1 (0.688) on utility rules is an artefact of near-trivial granulation (1\u20133 balls), not rho0.90-equivalent structure. Geometry distance to rho0.90 remains large (mean |\u0394|=313.3).",
    "q2_necessary_terms": "Necessary terms for rho0.90-equivalent structure: all per-split utility rules collapse to 1\u20133 balls (fail to granulate); purity-only accepts even fewer splits than boundary-only; minority survival term changes acceptance rate; fragmentation penalty further reduces splits (0.4 vs 1.7); rho0.90 actual (rule5) is the only config with realistic split depth (~240 splits). Classifier ranking among rho baselines: rho0.90 (0.597) > rho0.85 (0.582) > rho0.95 (0.569).",
    "q3_failing_assumption": "The failing assumption is **per-split greediness without global depth control**: utility rules accept 1.3 splits/run (near root-only), while rho0.95 over-splits at 268 (macro F1 0.569 vs rho0.90 0.597). Purity-gain gating rejects almost all KMeans proposals (weighted child purity rarely exceeds parent at a single step). Boundary-gain gating is similarly too conservative. What works is **stopping recursion near \u03c1\u22480.90** \u2014 a global depth/purity ceiling \u2014 not local accept/reject on individual split quality. Future algorithms need cumulative fragmentation budgets, not myopic per-split utility."
